WEBVTT

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:05.349
A math tutor has been working with a student for twelve lessons.

00:00:05.649 --> 00:00:07.904
All of it in the same conversation.

00:00:08.554 --> 00:00:12.786
In lesson one, she asked: don't show me the solution, just hints.

00:00:13.086 --> 00:00:14.565
Deal, said the tutor.

00:00:15.215 --> 00:00:18.825
In lesson six, for a review sheet, she asked the opposite.

00:00:19.125 --> 00:00:21.607
For this sheet, you can show the full solution.

00:00:22.257 --> 00:00:26.772
Now it's lesson twelve, and the history is almost ninety thousand tokens.

00:00:27.072 --> 00:00:29.226
She asks for help with question four.

00:00:29.526 --> 00:00:33.715
And the tutor switches between the two rules, with no clear logic.

00:00:34.365 --> 00:00:39.042
Nobody told the tutor that the lesson six rule applied only to that sheet.

00:00:39.342 --> 00:00:43.159
Nobody summarized what the student has already mastered.

00:00:43.459 --> 00:00:47.350
The model gets everything, and tries to guess what still holds.

00:00:47.650 --> 00:00:52.531
In this lesson, you'll learn to recognize this smell, and what to put in place of an

00:00:52.531 --> 00:00:53.751
ever growing history.

00:00:54.951 --> 00:00:55.897
Here's the cycle.

00:00:56.197 --> 00:00:57.674
A new turn arrives.

00:00:58.324 --> 00:01:01.806
Code appends it to the full history, which never shrinks.

00:01:02.456 --> 00:01:07.655
The model reads tens of thousands of tokens, most of them irrelevant, or stale.

00:01:08.305 --> 00:01:10.629
The answer gets worse, and costs more.

00:01:10.929 --> 00:01:14.963
And it goes into the history too, so the cycle continues.

00:01:15.613 --> 00:01:16.551
How do you spot it?

00:01:16.851 --> 00:01:20.384
The conversation, or the agent session, only grows.

00:01:20.684 --> 00:01:22.329
Nothing ever leaves.

00:01:22.979 --> 00:01:26.586
The model cites old information as if it were current.

00:01:26.886 --> 00:01:30.640
An order from three months ago, a file that has since changed.

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:35.655
Instructions given at the start get ignored, or mixed with later ones.

00:01:36.305 --> 00:01:40.293
Cost per turn climbs through the session, even for short questions.

00:01:40.593 --> 00:01:43.587
And so does the time until the first word appears.

00:01:44.237 --> 00:01:49.600
And answer quality drops in long sessions, while it's fine in short ones.

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:53.616
The default examples just append.

00:01:53.916 --> 00:01:57.820
The simplest chat code keeps the message list, and sends it all again.

00:01:58.120 --> 00:02:00.413
It works for the first ten turns.

00:02:01.063 --> 00:02:02.753
Large windows feel safe.

00:02:03.053 --> 00:02:06.846
If a million tokens fit, there seems to be no reason to cut.

00:02:07.146 --> 00:02:09.859
But fitting isn't the same as being used well.

00:02:10.159 --> 00:02:14.369
Models use what sits in the middle of a long context less reliably.

00:02:14.669 --> 00:02:19.934
And the lesson one and lesson six instructions fight in exactly that middle.

00:02:20.234 --> 00:02:21.379
The window fits.

00:02:21.679 --> 00:02:22.795
Attention doesn't.

00:02:23.445 --> 00:02:24.767
Deleting is scary.

00:02:25.067 --> 00:02:29.166
Without knowing what matters, keeping everything feels safer.

00:02:29.816 --> 00:02:32.130
And agents produce a lot of text.

00:02:32.430 --> 00:02:35.506
Tool results, files they read, test output.

00:02:35.806 --> 00:02:41.174
All of it enters the history, and stays there, long after it stopped being useful.

00:02:42.374 --> 00:02:44.149
The cost starts with quality.

00:02:44.449 --> 00:02:49.886
Rules and facts buried in the middle are followed less reliably, and contradictory

00:02:49.886 --> 00:02:52.405
information produces unstable answers.

00:02:53.055 --> 00:02:55.534
Then money, with illustrative numbers.

00:02:55.834 --> 00:03:00.553
A system prompt of two thousand tokens, and four hundred more each turn.

00:03:00.853 --> 00:03:05.475
At turn three, a call sends about three thousand two hundred tokens.

00:03:05.775 --> 00:03:08.770
At turn twelve, about six thousand eight hundred.

00:03:09.070 --> 00:03:11.572
At turn forty, about eighteen thousand.

00:03:12.222 --> 00:03:14.578
The student's question fits in one line.

00:03:14.878 --> 00:03:15.840
The bill doesn't.

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:20.955
Prompt caching makes a stable prefix cheaper, but history that changes every turn

00:03:20.955 --> 00:03:22.977
doesn't sit in that cached prefix.

00:03:23.277 --> 00:03:28.757
And at some point the session overflows the window, and the cut happens in the worst

00:03:28.757 --> 00:03:30.583
place, with no logic at all.

00:03:31.233 --> 00:03:34.353
The same thing shows up in a bank's WhatsApp support.

00:03:34.653 --> 00:03:38.740
In month one, the customer mentioned a cancelled instant payment.

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:43.475
In month three, they ask for their balance, and the bot still talks about that

00:03:43.475 --> 00:03:45.237
payment as the current problem.

00:03:45.537 --> 00:03:47.563
The new message was twelve words.

00:03:47.863 --> 00:03:50.036
The context held months of support.

00:03:51.236 --> 00:03:52.233
Here's one for you.

00:03:52.533 --> 00:03:57.122
A support chat always sends only the last ten messages, and drops the rest.

00:03:57.422 --> 00:04:02.385
Now customers complain that the bot forgets the order number they gave at the start.

00:04:02.685 --> 00:04:04.134
Is that context rot?

00:04:07.184 --> 00:04:07.849
No.

00:04:08.149 --> 00:04:10.726
The context isn't rotten, it's too short.

00:04:11.026 --> 00:04:14.810
Cutting by a fixed window fixes the cost, but loses facts.

00:04:15.110 --> 00:04:19.461
What's missing is explicit conversation state, a short term memory.

00:04:19.761 --> 00:04:24.552
The order number belongs there, outside the raw history, where your code keeps it up

00:04:24.552 --> 00:04:25.009
to date.

00:04:26.209 --> 00:04:27.854
There are two refactorings.

00:04:28.154 --> 00:04:33.123
When the session is a long conversation, replace the history with a summary.

00:04:33.423 --> 00:04:39.275
Old turns become a summary, and facts that must last, like preferences and agreed

00:04:39.275 --> 00:04:41.587
rules, move into explicit state.

00:04:42.237 --> 00:04:47.953
And when the context bloats with reading work, like files, pages or logs, extract a

00:04:47.953 --> 00:04:48.572
subagent.

00:04:48.872 --> 00:04:53.404
It reads in a clean context, and returns only the conclusion.

00:04:54.054 --> 00:04:56.945
Here's lesson twelve, before and after.

00:04:57.245 --> 00:05:02.668
Before, ninety thousand tokens of raw chat, with two rules in conflict, buried in

00:05:02.668 --> 00:05:03.405
the middle.

00:05:03.705 --> 00:05:08.433
After, a short summary, the state, with the student's level and the current rule,

00:05:08.433 --> 00:05:09.950
and the most recent turns.

00:05:10.250 --> 00:05:14.992
The active rule lives in one field, that you can read, edit and test.

00:05:15.642 --> 00:05:18.514
And watch for one mistake when you start cutting.

00:05:18.814 --> 00:05:21.430
Say you summarize every twenty turns.

00:05:21.730 --> 00:05:27.168
In month three, the summary still says: customer with a cancelled payment, treat as

00:05:27.168 --> 00:05:27.626
urgent.

00:05:27.926 --> 00:05:32.892
And the bot talks about it again, when the customer only asked for their balance.

00:05:33.192 --> 00:05:35.306
You only compacted the rot.

00:05:35.606 --> 00:05:37.486
A summary needs validity.

00:05:37.786 --> 00:05:41.261
What holds now goes into state, with a lifetime.

00:05:41.561 --> 00:05:46.017
And the past stays in the summary as past, or it leaves.

00:05:47.217 --> 00:05:49.014
One more, from the agent world.

00:05:49.314 --> 00:05:54.846
A coding agent read forty files, found the cause of a bug, and is now writing the

00:05:54.846 --> 00:05:55.256
patch.

00:05:55.556 --> 00:05:58.450
All forty files still go out on every turn.

00:05:58.750 --> 00:06:00.862
What's the most useful next step?

00:06:03.912 --> 00:06:10.173
Clear the old tool results, or move the reading into a subagent that returns only

00:06:10.173 --> 00:06:11.332
the conclusion.

00:06:11.632 --> 00:06:13.201
The files have done their job.

00:06:13.501 --> 00:06:18.349
Now they push the patch instruction into the middle of the context, where the model

00:06:18.349 --> 00:06:19.225
reads it worse.

00:06:19.525 --> 00:06:21.491
A bigger window doesn't fix that.

00:06:21.791 --> 00:06:26.194
And telling the model to ignore the files it won't use competes with tens of

00:06:26.194 --> 00:06:28.569
thousands of tokens that are still there.

00:06:28.869 --> 00:06:30.925
Code has to drop what's unused.

00:06:31.225 --> 00:06:32.633
Not the model.

00:06:33.833 --> 00:06:36.649
And one case that looks similar, but isn't.

00:06:36.949 --> 00:06:42.421
A system prompt of eight thousand tokens, that never changes between turns, in

00:06:42.421 --> 00:06:44.736
conversations of about six turns.

00:06:45.036 --> 00:06:46.776
Cost per message is high.

00:06:47.076 --> 00:06:49.760
But the context doesn't grow during the session.

00:06:50.060 --> 00:06:52.319
The weight is fixed in the prompt.

00:06:52.619 --> 00:06:53.953
That's prompt bloat.

00:06:54.253 --> 00:06:57.948
Trim the prompt, and if the start is stable, cache it.

00:06:59.148 --> 00:07:00.337
When can you ignore it?

00:07:00.637 --> 00:07:02.681
Sessions that are short by nature.

00:07:02.981 --> 00:07:06.719
A support chat that lasts five turns doesn't need compaction.

00:07:07.369 --> 00:07:10.342
Tasks where the whole input is the document.

00:07:10.642 --> 00:07:15.846
Reviewing one contract at once is different from carrying an old conversation.

00:07:16.146 --> 00:07:19.327
There, the text is the work, not leftover turns.

00:07:19.977 --> 00:07:20.863
And a prototype.

00:07:21.163 --> 00:07:24.996
Appending everything is the fastest way to test an idea.

00:07:25.296 --> 00:07:27.602
Just don't ship long sessions that way.

00:07:28.802 --> 00:07:31.188
So here's the idea to take with you.

00:07:31.488 --> 00:07:33.619
A long history isn't memory.

00:07:33.919 --> 00:07:38.252
It's a pile the model has to dig through on every turn, and pay for.

00:07:38.552 --> 00:07:44.150
Keep the facts that must last in explicit state, summarize the past as past, and let

00:07:44.150 --> 00:07:46.283
finished work leave the context.

00:07:46.933 --> 00:07:52.386
In the next lesson, we'll do the first refactoring step by step: replace history

00:07:52.386 --> 00:07:53.272
with summary.

00:07:53.572 --> 00:07:54.321
See you there.
